<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phalasarna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Falasarna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phalasarna was an ancient Greek harbour city at the western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, known for its enclosed artificial harbour, protected by walls and towers. It was a rival of Polyrrhenia and is thought to have served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. Uplift of the land, probably in the great earthquake of 365 CE, raised the harbour about six metres, leaving it now well inland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phalasarna.jpg" alt="Phalasarna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elpida Hadjidaki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phalasarna_Round_Tower_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city lay at the west end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. Its closed harbour, carved out of a lagoon, was ringed by stone quays with mooring stones and linked to the sea by artificial channels, one of which could be closed with a chain. Sandstone towers, bastions and hundreds of metres of walls protected the town. The acropolis stands on a cape rising 90 metres above the harbour, with a temple of the goddess Dictynna, towers, cisterns and watchtowers that could guard the sea routes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Graves show the site was inhabited in the 6th century BCE. Scylax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and Pliny wrote of it. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> with its own laws and coins, it was a maritime power, and according to Livy it sent military advisers and thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> to fight for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. A war with Polyrrhenia, probably over land, ended around 290 BCE through the mediation of Cleonymus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and its treaty, now in the museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kissamos">Kissamos</a>, shows that the citizens practised piracy in the 3rd century BCE. A second war, with Cydonia around 184 BCE, was settled by Roman intervention.</p><p>In 69–67 BCE Roman forces sent to suppress piracy stormed the city, blocked its harbour with masonry and destroyed it; no ancient source tells this directly, and the excavators' conclusion rests on traces of burning and the blockage. The site was forgotten until the British explorers Robert Pashley and T. A. B. Spratt found it in the 19th century. Spratt noted in 1859 that the harbour lay 100 yards from the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavations of the cemetery began in 1966 under Yannis Tzedakis, and of the town in 1986 under Elpida Hadjidaki, working with Frank J. Frost from 1988 to 1990. They have uncovered harbour towers, quays with bollards still bearing rope marks, a merchant's house, and a winery and warehouses for export; many finds are in the museums of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kissamos">Kisamos</a> and Chania. The valley is now farmland of olive groves and greenhouses, and its beaches draw visitors. The wreck of a British landing craft sunk on 28 May 1941, during the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, lies at the old channel entrance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Falasarna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Polyrrhenia controlled the west of Crete, with harbours at <b>Phalasarna</b> and Kisamos at times under its influence.</blockquote><a href="/state/polyrrhenia">Polyrrhenia</a></li></ul></section>
